    <h2>What Makes a Panel "Best" for the UK</h2>
    <p>The UK isn't sunny like Spain or California, so panel choice here comes down to three things: efficiency in low light, durability against wind and rain, and a strong warranty. Peak power output (kW) matters less than how well a panel performs on cloudy days, since the UK gets plenty of them.</p>

    <h2>Top Factors to Compare</h2>
    <ul>
      <li><strong>Efficiency rating:</strong> Look for panels rated 20%+ efficient. Higher efficiency means more power from the same roof space — useful if your roof is small or partially shaded.</li>
      <li><strong>Temperature coefficient:</strong> Counterintuitively, panels lose some efficiency in extreme heat. A low temperature coefficient (around -0.3%/°C or better) keeps performance more stable.</li>
      <li><strong>Warranty length:</strong> Look for at least a 25-year performance warranty and a 10-15 year product warranty. This is often a better sign of quality than the price tag.</li>
      <li><strong>Manufacturer reputation:</strong> UK installers most commonly fit panels from established brands with a strong track record of honouring warranty claims — not just the cheapest import available.</li>
    </ul>

    <h2>Monocrystalline vs Polycrystalline</h2>
    <p>Almost all panels sold in the UK today are monocrystalline — they're more efficient and have become similarly priced to older polycrystalline panels, which are now rarely used in residential installs.</p>

    <h2>What About Price?</h2>
    <p>A typical 3.5kW–4kW home solar system (enough for an average 3-bed house) costs roughly £5,000–£8,000 fully installed, panels included, before any government incentives. Price varies mainly by installer labour costs and panel brand, not hugely by "premium" vs "standard" panels — the efficiency gap between mid-range and top-tier panels is often only 1-2%.</p>

    <h2>Getting Quotes</h2>
    <p>The biggest overall cost driver isn't the panel brand — it's roof suitability (orientation, shading, roof angle) and installer pricing. It's worth getting 2-3 quotes from MCS-certified installers before deciding, since panel choice is often already narrowed down by what your installer stocks and recommends.</p>

    <h2>The Bottom Line</h2>
    <p>For most UK homes, any monocrystalline panel from a reputable brand with a 25-year warranty will perform well. Don't overpay for marginal efficiency gains — focus instead on getting a properly sized system for your roof and a trustworthy installer.</p>

